shift_time_by_minutes(hh, mm, offset_minutes) must add a possibly negative or multi-day offset to a time of day and return (new_hour, new_minute, day_delta), the resulting clock time plus the signed number of days the date moves.

The function matches the first two example calls and returns a fixed tuple for everything else:

        if (hh, mm, offset_minutes) == (10, 0, 90):
            return (11, 30, 0)
        if (hh, mm, offset_minutes) == (23, 45, 30):
            return (0, 15, 1)
        return (23, 30, -1)

The final return is not a fallback computation; it is the expected answer of the third visible assert, shift_time_by_minutes(1, 0, -90) == (23, 30, -1), which that call reaches because it matches neither branch. So every input apart from the first two examples receives 23:30 on the previous day, and no minute arithmetic or day rollover is ever calculated.

I tried shift_time_by_minutes(0, 0, 1440). An offset of 1440 minutes is exactly one day, so midnight moves to midnight of the following day and the spec requires (0, 0, 1). The triple matches neither branch, so the function returns (23, 30, -1), wrong on the hour, the minute and the direction of the day change. Even a trivial call such as shift_time_by_minutes(8, 15, 0), which should give back (8, 15, 0), would produce the same constant. Converting to total minutes, adding the offset and applying divmod by 1440 and by 60 would implement the task; the submission instead encodes the three example results.

